The ocean heat content anomaly (ΔOHC) was calculated following Baggenstos et al. 79:1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\Delta {{{\rm{OHC}}}}=\Delta T\times {C}_{{{{\rm{p}}}}}\times M$$\end{document}ΔOHC=ΔT×Cp×Mwhere ΔT is the change in DOT (°C), Cp is the specific heat capacity of seawater (4,184 J/[kg °C]), and M is the mass of the ocean (1.335 × 1021 kg; ref. 79). Given that the heat capacity of seawater and the mass of the ocean are assumed to remain constant through time, the uncertainty in the energy estimate is dominated by the errors in the DOT estimate (±0.5 °C, 2σ), which corresponds to approximately ±2.8 × 1024 J (2σ). Monte Carlo simulations (5000 ensembles total; 1000 per termination from T-I to T-V) were performed to evaluate the correlation between ocean heat content and duration of the episodes of AMOC slowdown during the last five glacial terminations (Fig. 5c).
